import{_ as t,o as s,c as a,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.D66Xq0Kv.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Network exits","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/network-exits.md","filePath":"guide/network-exits.md"}'),o={name:"guide/network-exits.md"};function n(r,e,d,h,l,c){return s(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="network-exits" tabindex="-1">Network exits <a class="header-anchor" href="#network-exits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Network exits&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A network exit is a computer of your workspace, such as a Mac, an Orange Pi or a mini PC at an office, that carries chosen outbound connections of your applications through its own internet connection. The destination sees that computer&#39;s public address instead of your server&#39;s. Uses: a partner API that only admits a known address, a stable address for an office or a branch, checking a site from another provider.</p><p>The device dials out to Orbit and keeps one connection open, so nothing is opened on it or on its router. Applications reach it through a proxy address that only works inside the cluster, and only to the destinations you allow.</p><p>What it is not: there is no address rotation and no shared pool of devices. Devices belong to one workspace.</p><h2 id="requirements" tabindex="-1">What you need <a class="header-anchor" href="#requirements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What you need {#requirements}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>A Mac with Apple silicon, or a Linux computer on ARM or x86 with systemd (Debian, Ubuntu, Armbian, Raspberry Pi OS and similar).</li><li>The device reaches your console&#39;s address over HTTPS.</li><li>An owner or admin of the workspace adds the device and makes the grants.</li></ul><p>The agent is a single binary of about 6.5 MB. Idle it holds about 8 MB of memory on Linux and 12 MB on a Mac, and each open connection costs well under 100 KB.</p><h2 id="guide" tabindex="-1">Your first exit, step by step <a class="header-anchor" href="#guide" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Your first exit, step by step {#guide}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Network exits → Step by step</strong> walks you to a working exit one decision at a time, and saves your place on the server: you can start on a laptop and continue on your phone, at the store, exactly where you stopped. The page also offers to continue a guide you left halfway.</p><ol><li><strong>Which internet should your application use?</strong> Your home&#39;s, your office&#39;s, or <strong>I just want to see how it works</strong>, which shows a simulation of the path and of a test result, marked as simulated, with nothing to install.</li><li><strong>Do you have a computer that can stay on all the time?</strong> A Mac, a Linux computer, or not yet.</li><li>Without one, choose how to get it: <strong>reuse</strong> an old laptop or desktop, <strong>buy</strong> a ready mini PC (an Intel N100 or N150 with 8 GB of RAM and a 128 GB SSD is plenty), or <strong>assemble</strong> an Orange Pi 5 or Raspberry Pi 5. Each choice shows what to ask for at the store and why, as a list you check off.</li><li><strong>Get it ready</strong>: keep a Mac awake and starting after a power failure, or install the system on a Linux computer and plug in the network cable.</li><li><strong>Connect it</strong>: the guide makes the pairing command and moves on by itself once the device is online.</li><li><strong>Test it</strong>: <strong>Test exit</strong> shows the address destinations will see.</li><li>From the last screen, <strong>Make a grant</strong> opens the grant form for a project.</li></ol><p>It takes six screens with a computer at hand and eight without one. Measured on a Debian 13 arm64 computer with systemd, on the same network as Orbit, the Linux command installed, paired and started the agent in under a second, and the device was online right after; over the internet, downloading the 6.5 MB agent adds a few seconds on a home connection.</p><h2 id="add" tabindex="-1">Add a device <a class="header-anchor" href="#add" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Add a device {#add}&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Open <strong>Network exits → Add device</strong>, give it a name and choose <strong>Make the command</strong>.</li><li>Choose where it runs and run the command the console shows in the device&#39;s terminal. It works once, within an hour. <ul><li><strong>Linux</strong> installs a systemd service under its own unprivileged user, <code>orbit-egress</code>, with its binary and key in <code>/var/lib/orbit-egress</code> (only that user can read them). It starts at boot and asks for <code>sudo</code> once if you are not root. <code>journalctl -u orbit-egress</code> shows what it does.</li><li><strong>Mac, at login</strong> installs a launchd agent in <code>~/.orbit-egress</code> that starts when you sign in, with no <code>sudo</code>.</li><li><strong>Mac, at boot</strong> starts before anyone signs in and comes back by itself after a restart. It asks for the Mac&#39;s password once.</li></ul></li><li>The command downloads the agent from your Orbit and checks its SHA-256 before running it, then trades the one-time token for the device&#39;s own key.</li><li>The device shows <strong>Online</strong>, with its system, agent version, open connections and traffic of the last hour.</li></ol><h2 id="test" tabindex="-1">Test the exit <a class="header-anchor" href="#test" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Test the exit {#test}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On the device&#39;s page, <strong>Test exit</strong> makes a connection through the device to your Orbit&#39;s own public address, the way an application&#39;s connection leaves, and shows the address that destination saw, the device and how long it took. If it fails, the page says why: the device is offline, paused or revoked, it could not find or reach the address, or the address is on a network the device keeps off. If Orbit itself sits behind a load balancer that replaces the address of whoever connects, the test says so instead of showing that internal address: the exit works, Orbit just cannot see what destinations see. Since 0.2.48 Traefik listens on the servers&#39; ports 80 and 443 itself, so a standard Orbit installation sees the real address.</p><h2 id="grants" tabindex="-1">Let a project use it <a class="header-anchor" href="#grants" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Let a project use it {#grants}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A grant lets a project&#39;s applications leave through chosen devices, to the destinations listed and nowhere else.</p><ol><li>Under <strong>Grants</strong>, choose <strong>New grant</strong>, pick the project, name the grant, check one or more devices and add the destinations.</li><li>A destination is a host and a port: an exact name (<code>api.partner.com:443</code>), an address (<code>203.0.113.7:8443</code>) or a leading wildcard (<code>*.partner.com:443</code>, which covers every name below <code>partner.com</code> and not <code>partner.com</code> itself).</li><li>With more than one device, each connection goes to the least busy device that is online.</li></ol><h2 id="use" tabindex="-1">Use it from an application <a class="header-anchor" href="#use" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Use it from an application {#use}&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>In the application, open <strong>Variables</strong>. Under <strong>Network exits for this project</strong>, pick the grant, keep the name <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> or change it, and choose <strong>Use the exit</strong>.</li><li>Apply the change and deploy.</li></ol><p>At deploy time the reference becomes the application&#39;s own proxy address, <code>http://&lt;application&gt;:&lt;credential&gt;@orbit-egress.orbit-system.svc:3128</code>. The credential belongs to that application alone and is derived from the grant, so removing the grant revokes it at once.</p><p>Give it to the HTTP client that should leave through the device. Most clients read <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code>, but then every HTTPS call of the application goes to the proxy and anything outside the grant gets <code>403</code>. To send only some calls, set it on that client:</p><div class="language-python vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">python</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">import</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> os</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">import</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> requests</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">proxy </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> os.environ[</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;HTTPS_PROXY&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">requests.get(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;https://api.partner.com/v1/orders&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">proxies</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">{</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;https&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: proxy})</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="language-js vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">js</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">import</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> { ProxyAgent, fetch } </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">from</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;undici&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">;</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">const</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> dispatcher</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> =</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> new</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ProxyAgent</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(process.env.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">HTTPS_PROXY</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">await</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> fetch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;https://api.partner.com/v1/orders&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, { dispatcher });</span></span></code></pre></div><p>HTTPS stays encrypted from the application to the destination: the device and Orbit carry bytes and never see the content.</p><h2 id="answers" tabindex="-1">What the application gets back <a class="header-anchor" href="#answers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the application gets back {#answers}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Answer to CONNECT</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The destination is outside the grant</td><td><code>403</code></td></tr><tr><td>The credential is unknown or belongs to another application</td><td><code>407</code></td></tr><tr><td>No device of the grant is online, or all are paused</td><td><code>503</code></td></tr><tr><td>The device could not reach the destination</td><td><code>502</code></td></tr><tr><td>The device did not open the connection within 10 seconds</td><td><code>504</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Nothing ever falls back to the server&#39;s own address.</p><h2 id="protections" tabindex="-1">Protections <a class="header-anchor" href="#protections" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Protections {#protections}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The device resolves names itself and refuses its own network: loopback, private, link-local, carrier-grade shared and reserved ranges. An application granted a partner&#39;s API never reaches the router, a printer or another machine next to the device.</li><li>The proxy only answers inside the cluster. A request that comes through the console&#39;s public address is turned away.</li><li>The agent updates itself when Orbit updates, checking the checksum Orbit announces, and waits for open connections to end before restarting.</li></ul><h2 id="control" tabindex="-1">Pause, revoke and remove <a class="header-anchor" href="#control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pause, revoke and remove {#control}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Pause</strong> closes the device&#39;s connections at once; new ones get <code>503</code> until you resume. The agent stays connected, so resuming takes effect at once.</li><li><strong>Revoke</strong> makes Orbit refuse the device&#39;s key at once and disconnects it. The device stays listed; <strong>New pairing command</strong> brings it back.</li><li><strong>Remove</strong> deletes the device and takes it out of every grant. The agent stays installed on the device until you remove it there: <ul><li>Linux: <code>sudo systemctl disable --now orbit-egress &amp;&amp; sudo rm -rf /etc/systemd/system/orbit-egress.service /var/lib/orbit-egress &amp;&amp; sudo userdel orbit-egress</code></li><li>Mac: <code>launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.getcortexlabs.orbit-egress; rm -rf ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.getcortexlabs.orbit-egress.plist ~/.orbit-egress</code> (for an agent installed at boot, <code>sudo launchctl bootout system/com.getcortexlabs.orbit-egress</code> and remove <code>/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.getcortexlabs.orbit-egress.plist</code>).</li></ul></li></ul>`,32)])])}const u=t(o,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
